  "brief_md": "# P05 — Customizable Lightweight Local Newspapers\n\n## Idea\nA way to configure a local print (or digital) newspaper to include only topics the reader cares about, making it lighter/shorter. Source: HN comment (https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45500937).\n\n## Payer\nIndividual readers are the plausible payer. Price anchor: Edition.ink offers a free tier and a **$5/month Pro tier** (no ads/tracking) — https://edition.ink/pricing. A secondary, unvalidated payer hypothesis is local publishers licensing a personalization layer to cut newsprint costs; no evidence of publisher willingness-to-pay was found.\n\n## Competitors\n- **Edition.ink** (direct) — Free + $5/mo Pro, AI-personalized daily newspaper delivered by email/PDF/print. https://edition.ink/pricing\n- **Newspaper Club** (adjacent) — per-copy print runs from 1 to 1000s, no fixed price list. https://www.newspaperclub.com/\n- **MakeMyNewspaper** (adjacent) — print runs from 50 copies at $99, digital from $19. https://makemynewspaper.com/prices\n- **Happiedays/Lettr** (adjacent) — per-copy consumer newspaper maker, calculator-based pricing. https://www.happiedays.com/en_eu\n- **Faxx.ai** (adjacent) — free AI-personalized local news via SMS. https://faxx.ai/\n- **Open-source hobby clones** (OpenPaper, Morning Paper, Editions, Yournalist) — free, self-hosted single-reader personalized newspapers, several built by solo developers with AI agents in 2026, directly validating both the demand and the ease of building this with one person + AI tools. https://github.com/falense/openpaper, https://github.com/dmthepm/morning-paper\n\n## Acquisition channel\n- **Nextdoor neighborhood ads**: $2–5 CPC, $3–10/day minimum. https://nearleadsgrow.com/how-much-does-nextdoor-advertising-cost/\n- **Facebook/Instagram local ads**: ~$0.70–1.14 average CPC (2025/2026 benchmarks). https://www.wordstream.com/blog/facebook-ads-benchmarks-2025\n- Direct publisher partnership channel: plausible but no cost data found [unsourced].\n\n## Demand evidence\nMultiple decades-old reader complaints about unwanted sections/content bloat corroborate the seed pain independently of the HN quote:\n- \"So I'm asking myself if it's worth paying $130 just to get six months of crossword puzzles and comics, which are the parts of the paper I seem to find most interesting.\" — Concord Monitor letter (https://www.concordmonitor.com/2017/11/06/monitor-is-not-worth-it-13537097/)\n- \"Trying to fit multiple content into only 2 sections makes reading nonsensical... all the generic content in lieu of local reporters.\" — Napa Valley Register letter (https://napavalleyregister.com/opinion/letters/letter-disappointed-with-new-format/article_db7154ee-390b-11ed-ac71-8bf632ab8a4e.html)\n- \"The tiny Life section is not interesting to us, and we toss the Sports section in the recycle bin straightaway.\" — LA Observed reader letter (http://www.laobserved.com/archive/2008/03/ranting_about_long_beach.php)\n\n## Case for\nA solo builder with AI agents can plausibly ship this in six weeks — open-source single-developer clones (OpenPaper, Morning Paper, Editions) already prove the core pipeline (ingest → classify/rewrite via LLM → render edition) is buildable fast and cheaply. The $5/month Edition.ink price validates some willingness to pay at a low price point, and the reader complaint pattern is a recurring, multi-decade pain, not a fad. A digital-only MVP sidesteps print/distribution capital costs and can be tested cheaply via hyperlocal ad channels (Nextdoor, Facebook) in a single zip code.\n\n## Case against\nThe niche is already crowded with both a funded direct competitor (Edition.ink) and multiple free, open-source, single-developer tools solving the identical problem — eroding the differentiation a solo founder could claim. The 'local' angle is the hardest part: clean structured local feeds are scarce as local papers shrink, and the core mechanic (scraping and AI-rewriting third-party news articles) sits squarely inside active copyright litigation against AI companies by news publishers (NYT/Microsoft/OpenAI; Advance Local Media/Cohere). Local news paid-subscription behavior is structurally declining (only ~9% of Americans pay for local news per Pew-cited data), raising doubt about a sustainable consumer-payer base, and the print-specific framing in the seed quote implies physical unit economics that are unlikely to beat incumbent print services already offering sub-$2/copy pricing at volume.\n\n## Open questions\n- Does the real customer need *print*, or is digital sufficient — and if print, do the unit economics work at consumer scale?\n- Is AI rewriting of scraped news content legally viable given ongoing copyright litigation on point?\n- Do local publishers want this (license opportunity) or fear it (cannibalization)? No publisher-side demand evidence found.\n- How does a six-week solo product differentiate from free existing open-source equivalents?\n- What is realistic CAC for non-technical local-news readers, versus the tech-savvy early adopters who already self-build this with Claude Code plugins?\n"
